  "mode": "verify",
  "problem": "Find ${(g\\cdot {f}^{-1})}^{\u2032}(3)$ of the product of functions, given the following table of values of $f(x),{f}^{\u2032}(x),g(x),$ and ${g}^{\u2032}(x)$ at some points.\n\n| **$x$** | $f(x)$ | ${f}^{\u2032}(x)$ | $g(x)$ | ${g}^{\u2032}(x)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $3$ | $-1$ | $2$ | $4$ | $0$ |\n| $-2$ | $3$ | $4$ | $1$ | $-1$ |\n\n${(g\\cdot {f}^{-1})}^{\u2032}(3)=$\n{{field-1}}",
  "worked_solution": "The formula for the derivative of the inverse ${f}^{-1}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n({f}^{-1})^{\u2032}(x)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{\u2032}({f}^{-1}(x))}.\n$$\n\nUsing this, and the product rule, the derivative of the function $g(x){f}^{-1}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n(g\\cdot {f}^{-1})^{\u2032}(x)={g}^{\u2032}(x){f}^{-1}(x)+\\frac{g(x)}{{f}^{\u2032}({f}^{-1}(x))}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=3,$ we have\n\n$$\n(g\\cdot {f}^{-1})^{\u2032}(3)={g}^{\u2032}(3){f}^{-1}(3)+\\frac{g(3)}{{f}^{\u2032}({f}^{-1}(3))}.\n$$\n\nFrom the table, we obtain ${f}^{-1}(3)=-2,$ $g(3)=4,$ ${g}^{\u2032}(3)=0,$ and ${f}^{\u2032}(-2)=4.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(g\\cdot {f}^{-1})^{\u2032}(3) & ={g}^{\u2032}(3){f}^{-1}(3)+\\frac{g(3)}{{f}^{\u2032}({f}^{-1}(3))} \\\\  & =0\\cdot (-2)+\\frac{4}{{f}^{\u2032}(-2)} \\\\  & =0+\\frac{4}{4} \\\\  & =0+1 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
